    "description": "For State of Euphoria, Charlie Benante played a Tama Granstar kit — the line that bridged the Artstar II era of Among the Living and the Tama Artstar Custom configurations he would adopt heading into the 1990s. The Granstar shared the Artstar's birch shell philosophy but used slightly different bearing edges and hardware, producing a tone that was a touch brighter and more aggressive in the upper midrange — well-suited to the dry, forward Compass Point drum sound that Mark Dodson was after.\n\nTwo 22\" bass drums remained the foundation. Charlie had been an early adopter of dual kick drums in thrash, and although his right-foot single-bass technique was so developed that many of his most famous patterns were executed on a single drum, the second kick gave him symmetric tonal weight and let him voice longer sustained passages without compromising stroke consistency. The 22\" diameter delivered the punchy, focused low-end that cuts cleanly through Scott Ian and Dan Spitz's guitar layers.\n\nThe tom array — three rack toms plus a single 16\" floor tom — was tighter than the Among the Living configuration and reflected the more compact compositional approach of State of Euphoria's songs. The 10/12/13 progression gave Charlie melodic options for fills without slowing transitions, and the single floor tom produced the focused low-end thump that anchors the album's biggest accent points.\n\nTama's birch shells were essential to the Compass Point drum sound. Birch naturally rolls off some low-midrange \"boom\" while emphasising the attack transient and upper-midrange definition that microphones love, which is why the toms on State of Euphoria sound so crisp and articulated even in dense mixes.",
